# Git for Blog Posts: A Beginner's Tutorial

## Basic Terminal Navigation

Before touching git, you need to move around your files:

- ls — list files in your current directory
- cd <folder> — change into a folder (e.g., cd my-blog)
- cd .. — go up one directory
- pwd — shows your current directory path

## The Git Workflow for Updating a Blog Post

### 1. Check where you stand
bash
git status

This tells you which files have been modified, added, or are untracked. Run this often — it's your compass.

### 2. See what you changed
bash
git diff <file>

Shows the exact lines you added (prefixed with +) and removed (prefixed with -). *Note:* This only works for files that already exist in the repository. New untracked files won't show a diff. If the output is long, git opens it in a pager. Navigate with:
- *Enter* — scroll down one line
- *Space* — scroll down one full page
- *b* — scroll up one page
- *q* — exit back to your terminal

### 3. Undo mistakes
bash
git restore <file>

This *discards* your uncommitted changes and reverts the file to the last committed version. Use with caution — those changes are gone.

### 4. Stage your changes
bash
git add <file>

This tells git "I want to include this file in my next commit." You can git add multiple files before committing.

### 5. Commit your changes
bash
git commit -m "Updated blog post about deploying with Docker"

Saves a snapshot with a message. Write messages that your future self will understand.

### 6. Pull before pushing (avoid conflicts)
bash
git pull origin <repo>

Downloads any changes from the remote repository and merges them into your local copy. Always do this before pushing, especially if others edit the same repo.

### 7. Push your changes
bash
git push origin <repo>

Uploads your commits to the remote repository (GitHub, GitLab, etc.).

---

## Typical Session: Update a Blog Post

bash
cd ~/projects/my-blog
git pull origin main                # get latest changes before you start
git status                          # see what's changed
git diff posts/my-new-post.md       # review your edits (Enter/Space to scroll, q to exit)
git add posts/my-new-post.md        # stage the file
git commit -m "Added new post"      # commit
git push origin main                # push to remote


