## Glossary

- *Commit*: A saved snapshot of your project at a specific point in time, like saving your progress in a video game. It includes a message describing what changed.
- *Directory*: Another word for a folder on your computer where files are stored.
- *Diff*: Short for "difference." It shows exactly what lines were added or removed in a file compared to the last saved version.
- *Origin*: The default name for the remote server where your project lives (like GitHub or GitLab).
- *Pager*: A built-in tool that lets you read long text outputs one screen at a time, like reading a book page by page instead of all at once.
- *Pull*: Downloading the latest changes from the remote server to your local computer, ensuring you're working with the most up-to-date version.
- *Push*: Uploading your local commits to the remote server so others (or your live website) can see them.
- *Repository (repo)*: The project folder that Git is tracking. It holds all your files and the entire history of changes made to them.
- *Stage / Staging*: The prep area. When you stage a file, you're telling Git, "I want to include this specific change in my next snapshot (commit)."
- *Untracked*: Files that Git sees in your folder but hasn't started keeping history for yet (like a brand new blog post you just created).
